    {
      "id": "calc-ab-bank-u5-001",
      "status": "draft",
      "unit": "unit-5",
      "unitLabel": "Analytical Applications of Differentiation",
      "topic": "Intervals of increase",
      "skills": ["analysis"],
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "calculator": "prohibited",
      "stem": "A differentiable function \\(g\\) satisfies \\(g'(x)=x^2-4\\). On which interval is \\(g\\) increasing?",
      "choices": [
        { "key": "A", "text": "\\((-2,2)\\) only" },
        { "key": "B", "text": "\\(( -\\infty,-2)\\) and \\((2,\\infty)\\)" },
        { "key": "C", "text": "\\(( -\\infty,2)\\) only" },
        { "key": "D", "text": "\\(( -\\infty,\\infty)\\)" }
      ],
      "answer": "B",
      "explanation": "The function increases where \\(g'(x)>0\\). Since \\(x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)\\), the derivative is positive when \\(x<-2\\) or \\(x>2\\).",
