    {
      "id": "calc-ab-bank-u6-001",
      "status": "draft",
      "unit": "unit-6",
      "unitLabel": "Integration and Accumulation of Change",
      "topic": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
      "skills": ["procedural", "translation"],
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "calculator": "prohibited",
      "stem": "Let \\(F(x)=\\int_1^{x^2}\\sqrt{1+t^3}\\,dt\\). Which expression is equal to \\(F'(x)\\)?",
      "choices": [
        { "key": "A", "text": "\\(\\sqrt{1+x^6}\\)" },
        { "key": "B", "text": "\\(2x\\sqrt{1+x^3}\\)" },
        { "key": "C", "text": "\\(2x\\sqrt{1+x^6}\\)" },
        { "key": "D", "text": "\\(x^2\\sqrt{1+x^6}\\)" }
      ],
      "answer": "C",
      "explanation": "The Fundamental Theorem gives the integrand evaluated at \\(x^2\\), then the chain rule multiplies by \\(2x\\): \\(F'(x)=\\sqrt{1+(x^2)^3}(2x)\\).",
